WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.380
Can a flash make a neuron fire on cue?

2
00:00:03.640 --> 00:00:08.240
Watch the flash above this brain cell and the electrical recording beside it.

3
00:00:09.220 --> 00:00:13.580
That sharp peak is a spike, the brief electrical signal a neuron sends.

4
00:00:14.750 --> 00:00:16.890
The light gets its target from a gene.

5
00:00:18.350 --> 00:00:22.790
Give selected neurons the gene for a light-sensitive protein, called an opsin.

6
00:00:24.110 --> 00:00:28.390
Those neurons make the protein, which sits in the membrane, the cell's thin

7
00:00:28.390 --> 00:00:29.390
boundary.

8
00:00:29.950 --> 00:00:35.010
One opsin, channelrhodopsin-2, is a channel that opens under blue light.

9
00:00:36.210 --> 00:00:39.410
When it opens, particles with a positive charge flow in.

10
00:00:40.770 --> 00:00:45.170
Charged particles are ions, and their positive charge makes the inside less

11
00:00:45.170 --> 00:00:49.410
negative. That change brings the neuron closer to firing.

12
00:00:50.583 --> 00:00:55.443
When the voltage reaches the level needed to fire, a spike shoots along the neuron.

13
00:00:56.763 --> 00:01:00.063
Flash after flash, watch for spikes in step with the light.

14
00:01:01.263 --> 00:01:04.143
The recording tells you whether the cell actually followed.

15
00:01:05.267 --> 00:01:08.247
A different opsin can quiet the same kind of cell.

16
00:01:09.707 --> 00:01:15.167
Halorhodopsin is a pump that uses yellow light to move negative chloride ions into

17
00:01:15.167 --> 00:01:21.007
the neuron. That negative charge keeps voltage below firing and can stop spikes

18
00:01:21.007 --> 00:01:22.007
while light shines.

19
00:01:23.017 --> 00:01:27.797
With the gene choosing the cells and light choosing the moment, you can test what

20
00:01:27.797 --> 00:01:28.877
their activity causes.

21
00:01:30.217 --> 00:01:31.737
But the beam reaches tissue too.

22
00:01:33.457 --> 00:01:37.277
Suppose shining light makes the spikes on your recording become less frequent.

23
00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:42.300
Now keep the light the same and look at tissue without the opsin.

24
00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:46.260
What would fewer spikes there tell you about the light itself?

25
00:01:51.983 --> 00:01:56.283
Fewer spikes without the opsin would mean light alone can change firing.

26
00:01:57.643 --> 00:02:02.763
In mice, Owen, Liu and Kreitzer found that continuous light could suppress spikes

27
00:02:02.763 --> 00:02:08.643
without an opsin. Light can warm tissue, so compare recordings under matched light.

28
00:02:09.683 --> 00:02:13.723
Even with an opsin, check how the selected cells actually respond.

29
00:02:15.403 --> 00:02:16.523
Record while the light is on.

30
00:02:17.843 --> 00:02:22.443
Prolonged excitation can hold voltage too high for the cell to reset, so spikes

31
00:02:22.443 --> 00:02:26.103
stop. Keep recording after the light ends, too.

32
00:02:27.503 --> 00:02:32.983
Abruptly ending silencing can trigger rebound firing, extra spikes right after the

33
00:02:32.983 --> 00:02:33.983
light turns off.

34
00:02:34.767 --> 00:02:38.007
Recordings can show how closely spikes follow the flashes.

35
00:02:39.467 --> 00:02:44.927
In 2005, Edward Boyden and his colleagues tested brain cells grown in culture.

36
00:02:46.247 --> 00:02:50.527
They gave these hippocampal neurons the gene for channelrhodopsin-2.

37
00:02:51.747 --> 00:02:55.527
The cells fired in step with millisecond flashes of blue light.

38
00:02:56.550 --> 00:03:01.710
With light driving cells, researchers could test what activating them causes in an

39
00:03:01.710 --> 00:03:08.510
animal. In 2007, Adamantidis and colleagues delivered light through a fibre to

40
00:03:08.510 --> 00:03:09.970
activate mouse brain cells.

41
00:03:11.230 --> 00:03:12.910
These were hypocretin neurons.

42
00:03:14.270 --> 00:03:18.010
Mice woke more often from slow-wave sleep and from REM sleep.
